<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_from-structures-to-classes">
  <title>From Structures to Classes</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous sections, you used <c>struct</c> to group related data
      and behavior into one type.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
    void double_size();
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      C++ also provides another user-defined type called a <term>class</term>.
      A class can also contain data members and member functions.
    </p>

    <p>
      In fact, <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c> are very closely related in C++.
      One important difference is the default access given to their members.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Classes and Objects</title>

    <p>
      A <term>class</term> is a user-defined type that describes a category of
      objects. The class specifies the data those objects contain and the
      operations they can perform.
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful analogy is to think of a class as a blueprint. The blueprint
      describes how objects of that class are organized, while each object is a
      separate instance created from that description.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, a <c>Rectangle</c> class can describe the data and behavior
      shared by all rectangle objects:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We can then create different objects of the same class:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
Rectangle table{6.0, 3.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Both <c>room</c> and <c>table</c> are objects of the
      <c>Rectangle</c> class. They have the same kinds of data and behavior,
      but each object has its own state.
    </p>

    <p>
      An object created from a class is also called an
      <term>instance</term> of that class.
    </p>

    <p>
      The class describes what all objects of that type have in common; each
      object stores its own values for its data members.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Struct and a Class Can Look Very Similar</title>

    <p>
      Consider the familiar <c>Rectangle</c> structure:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We can write a very similar type using <c>class</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The keyword <c>public</c> means that code outside the class may access
      these members.
    </p>

    <p>
      With <c>public:</c> written explicitly, this class can be used in much
      the same way as the earlier structure:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

std::cout << room.length << '\n';
std::cout << room.calculate_area() << '\n';
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Both <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c> can therefore contain:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>data members</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>member functions</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Default Access</title>

    <p>
      The main difference we need to know right now is the default access of
      members.
    </p>

    <p>
      Members of a <c>struct</c> are <term>public</term> by default.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      This means that code outside the structure may access the members:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

room.length = 15.0;
room.width = 10.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Members of a <c>class</c>, however, are
      <term>private</term> by default.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      In this version, code outside the class cannot directly access
      <c>length</c> or <c>width</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;

room.length = 15.0;   // error
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We will study public and private members in more detail in the next
      section.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Making Class Members Public</title>

    <p>
      If we want class members to be accessible from outside the class, we can
      use the <c>public:</c> access label.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Now the data members can be accessed using the dot operator:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

room.length = 15.0;
room.width = 10.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The <c>public:</c> label applies to the members that follow it until
      another access label appears or the class definition ends.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double length;
    double width;

    double calculate_area() const;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Here, both data members and the member function are public.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Why Use a Class?</title>

    <p>
      If structures can already contain both data and member functions, why
      would we use a class?
    </p>

    <p>
      One important reason is that a class can control how code outside the
      class accesses and changes its data.
    </p>

    <p>
      Consider a rectangle whose dimensions should never be negative. If the
      data members are public, any part of the program can write:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.length = -5.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The object now contains a value that may not make sense for the problem
      being modeled.
    </p>

    <p>
      A class can hide some data from outside code and provide member functions
      that control how the object's state is changed.
    </p>

    <p>
      This idea is called <term>encapsulation</term>. We will begin exploring
      it in the next section.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>struct or class?</title>

    <p>
      In C++, both <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c> are capable of representing
      objects with state and behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful beginner guideline is:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use a <c>struct</c> when the type is primarily a simple collection
          of related data and direct access to that data is appropriate.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Use a <c>class</c> when the type should control access to its internal
          state and provide a public interface for using the object.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      These are design guidelines rather than strict language rules. C++ allows
      both structures and classes to contain data members and member functions.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Classes as Abstractions</title>

    <p>
    A class can provide more than a way to group data and functions. It can
    also define a clear boundary between how an object is used and how it is
    implemented internally.
  </p>

  <p>
    Code outside the class can work through a set of public operations, while
    some of the object's internal data can remain hidden.
  </p>

  <p>
    This creates an <term>abstraction</term>: users of the class can focus on
    what the object can do without needing to know every detail of how it
    stores and manages its state.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keeping data private is an important part of this design because it allows
    the class to control how its state is changed. In the next section, we
    will examine public and private members in detail.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_from_structures_to_classes_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which statement about C++ structures and classes is true?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Only classes can contain member functions.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Both structures and classes can contain member functions.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Both structures and classes can contain data members and member
              functions.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              In C++, both can represent objects with state and behavior.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Structures can contain data, while classes can contain only
              functions.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Both structures and classes can contain data and functions.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_from_structures_to_classes_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the default access for members of a <c>struct</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>public</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Members of a structure are public by default.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>private</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Members of a class are private by default.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>const</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>const</c> describes whether something may be modified; it is
              not an access level.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_from_structures_to_classes_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the default access for members of a <c>class</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>public</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Public is the default for a structure, not a class.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>private</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Members of a class are private by default.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>protected</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>protected</c> is another access level, but it is not the
              default for a class.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_from_structures_to_classes_4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why might a programmer choose a class instead of exposing all data
          members publicly?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Because structures cannot contain functions.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Structures can contain member functions in C++.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              To control how outside code accesses and changes the object's
              state.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Controlling access to internal state is an important reason for
              using classes.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Because class objects do not contain data members.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Classes can contain data members just like structures.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_from_structures_to_classes_5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which type is the stronger candidate for a <c>class</c> according to
          the design guideline in this section?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              A <c>Point</c> that simply stores public <c>x</c> and <c>y</c>
              coordinates.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A small type whose purpose is primarily to group related data is
              often a reasonable use of a <c>struct</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              A <c>Thermostat</c> that should control how its temperature
              setting is changed.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A class is a natural choice when the type should provide
              operations that control access to its internal state.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Any type that contains at least one member function.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Structures can contain member functions too, so the presence of
              a member function alone does not require a class.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_from_structures_to_classes_6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does it mean to say that a class provides an
          <term>abstraction</term>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Every detail of the object's representation must be visible to
              code that uses it.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Abstraction allows users of a type to work through its interface
              without depending on every implementation detail.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Outside code can focus on what the object can do without needing
              to know every detail of how its state is stored.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The public operations describe how to use the object while
              implementation details can remain hidden.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              All objects of the class share one copy of their data.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Each object has its own state. Abstraction concerns the boundary
              between how an object is used and how it is implemented.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
